WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.160
MICHELLE (off-screen):
There's a country on
planet earth that's about

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.960
the same size
as the United States,

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.960
but it has around
four times as many people.

4
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.600
Still, its wild lands are
so vast with creatures seen

5
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.160
nowhere else, incredible
animals that survive in

6
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.960
some of the most
extreme landscapes,

7
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.800
like the highest and largest
mountain region in the world.

8
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.800
With so much ice it's
known as the Third Pole.

9
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.920
This country is famous for grass
that grows as big as trees.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.440
And for an animal that
will eat that giant grass

11
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.680
all day long.

12
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.680
These are
<i>The Hidden Kingdoms of China.</i>

13
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.640
This is the fastest
growing plant in the world.

14
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.880
Bamboo.

15
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.480
It can grow around
a meter a day.

16
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.480
And some of it as tall
and as thick as a tree.

17
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.280
China is the
kingdom of Bamboo.

18
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.840
Ruled by a queen,

19
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.000
the giant panda.

20
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.040
This country
is her native home.

21
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.680
She's China's national animal

22
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.680
and she happens to
eat a lot of bamboo.

23
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.960
The giant panda
has the tools for it.

24
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.600
With five claws on each
paw and a padded wrist bone

25
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.920
that works like a thumb.

26
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.000
She can grip it
almost like a monkey.

27
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.880
Big and powerful jaw muscles
help her grind up the tough

28
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:24.880
stems and give her that
recognizable round face.

29
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.640
A few million years ago,

30
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.880
the pandas' ancestors
preferred to eat more meat.

31
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.440
These days they're
mostly vegetarian.

32
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.400
Bamboo is low in nutrients,
so the average bear needs to eat

33
00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.680
a lot of it and
for a long time,

34
00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.680
around 12 hours every day.

35
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.000
Perhaps that's the reason
for this panda mother's

36
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.600
big, round belly.

37
00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.120
Or maybe it's because
she just gave birth

38
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.040
to her very tiny panda prince.

39
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.240
At only two weeks old,
he weighs half a kilogram.

40
00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.560
A newborn panda is one of the
smallest mammals born compared

41
00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.280
to the size of the parent.

42
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.960
They're also born blind
so this little one needs

43
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.800
his mom for everything.

44
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.920
For the next two years, she'll
stick by his side and teach

45
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.680
him all that she knows until
the day he's big enough to

46
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.240
strike out on his own.

47
00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.840
Thousands of meters higher up,
in an all-together different

48
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:04.840
type of forest there lives
another very special creature.

49
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.680
That is also unique to China.

50
00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.800
The golden snub-nosed
monkey only lives here in the

51
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.760
high mountain
forests of central China.

52
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.680
Up to 4,000 meters
above sea level.

53
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.280
This is the world's
largest high-altitude forest,

54
00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.440
where there are more tree
species than in any other

55
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.600
deciduous forest on earth.

56
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.920
It's spring!

57
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.120
And these monkeys are
making the most of it.

58
00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:59.840
This troop has five families.

59
00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.640
Each family has one
male and several females
with their young.

60
00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.400
Males that don't belong
to these families hang out

61
00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:15.400
together in a
monkey bachelor gang.

62
00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.840
They're an unruly bunch,
but for now,

63
00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:24.960
the focus is not on them.

64
00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:29.880
It's on the latest
addition to the family,

65
00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:34.000
a brand new, baby girl.

66
00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:41.240
She happens to be the
first born this spring

67
00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:47.120
so all the females in
the family want a cuddle.

68
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.520
This aunty is first.

69
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.160
(squeaking).

70
00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.120
Then aunty number
two wants a hold.

71
00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:20.640
(squeaking).

72
00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.240
And aunty number three.

73
00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.040
(squeaking).

74
00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.800
Enough! It's mom time.

75
00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.080
Meaning lunchtime.

76
00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:58.600
In fact, all the
monkeys want lunch.

77
00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:04.120
At this time of year,
they spend a quarter of

78
00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:08.760
daylight hours eating
the fresh, new leaves.

79
00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:16.040
A good source of protein
and their favorite food.

80
00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.640
As temperatures rise,
the monkeys follow the leaves

81
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.760
as they spring to life,

82
00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.840
further and further
up the mountain.

83
00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:39.320
But eventually
the trees stop growing.

84
00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.760
And at around 5,000 meters
above sea level we hit.

85
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.440
The great plains of China.

86
00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.160
Part of the biggest
grassland on earth,

87
00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:57.880
it covers a
third of the country.

88
00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:04.320
For nine months of the year,
it's packed with snow and ice.

89
00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:08.160
But there are a couple
of months when it transforms

90
00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.440
into a hidden
kingdom of plenty.

91
00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.440
Precious time
to raise a family.

92
00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:21.040
And up here there's a
family guy who takes the prize

93
00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.000
for dad of the year.

94
00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.560
The Tibetan
fox is no lazy dog.

95
00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.880
But he has no idea
what lies ahead in

96
00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:39.720
this fast-changing wilderness.

97
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.960
After a very long winter,
the residents of China's

98
00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:50.360
great plains are waking up.

99
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.480
The father fox
has a family of five.

100
00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.360
Four cubs and mother fox too.

101
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:03.000
After a month underground,
they are just emerging from

102
00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.680
their den with energy to burn.

103
00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:16.320
Tibetan foxes normally only
give birth to two or three cubs,

104
00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.160
so four is a handful.

105
00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.440
And like most baby foxes,

106
00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.160
they're completely dependent
on both parents for survival.

107
00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.360
(yipping).

108
00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.480
They need constant feeding.

109
00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.080
Dad and mom take turns.

110
00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.120
Today, father fox
stays home to babysit.

111
00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:44.200
And mother heads
out for the hunt.

112
00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:53.280
To be safe, the cubs
stay close to the den.

113
00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.280
While the other
grassland creatures
go about their business.

114
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.440
Some more edible than others.

115
00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:05.720
(howling).

116
00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.440
(yipping).

117
00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:12.800
With this many cubs, someone
is bound to get left out.

118
00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.240
As the three
brothers grow stronger,

119
00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:21.800
their smaller sister will
need some extra attention.

120
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.160
Beyond the grasslands,
lies the wildest,

121
00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.880
highest and largest
mountain region on Earth.

122
00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:38.440
This is China's
mountain kingdom,

123
00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:43.040
home to some of the
world's most elusive animals.

124
00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.720
Including these
goofy looking creatures.

125
00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.280
The Himalayan marmot,

126
00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.040
sometimes known as
the Tibetan snow pig.

127
00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.400
They're in the
squirrel family

128
00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:02.200
but are more the
size of a groundhog.

129
00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:07.960
Marmots are also one
of the highest living
mammals in the world.

130
00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.760
They survive the coldest
eight months of the year

131
00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.040
by sleeping in their burrows

132
00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.400
and dropping their heart beats
to just a few per minute.

133
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:30.760
But right now, they're feasting
to rebuild their fat reserves.

134
00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:41.240
This mountain habitat
sits within an area nearly

135
00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.560
14 times the size
of Yellowstone.

136
00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:50.200
A place where four
seasons can pass in one day.

137
00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:54.040
And after the
North and South Pole,

138
00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.840
it ranks third for ice.

139
00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:03.360
Ice that eventually feeds
Asia's three mightiest rivers,

140
00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:07.120
the Yellow,
Mekong and Yangtze.

141
00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:12.880
Water for most of the
billion people that live below.

142
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.160
Nestled in the western
province of Qinghai,

143
00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:24.760
this remote and extreme
world is home to a sacred and

144
00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:28.200
most elusive predator.

145
00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:32.800
Only when she
moves can we see her.

146
00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:36.280
It's the ghost of
the mountain kingdom.

147
00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.760
Something rarely
caught on camera.

148
00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:46.240
MICHELLE (off-screen): China is
home to more snow leopards than

149
00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.960
anywhere else on the planet.

150
00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:54.200
At around 16,000
feet above sea level,

151
00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:59.720
they're perfectly
adapted to a world that
is inaccessible to most.

152
00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:06.400
Their individual territories
with steep sided mountains and

153
00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:10.160
exposed valleys are huge,

154
00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.520
but this female
still marks hers.

155
00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.120
She's the apex
predator all the way up here.

156
00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:29.640
And when there are at best,

157
00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:32.840
7,000 snow leopards
left on planet earth,

158
00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:36.680
it's a privileged
position to be in.

159
00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:46.720
This snow leopard is also
a mother of this yearling cub.

160
00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:52.640
She takes the
time to play with him,

161
00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:57.520
a mountain version
of hide and seek.

162
00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:02.280
Vital lessons
disguised as a game.

163
00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:12.320
We are lucky to witness a
snow leopard family in the wild.

164
00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:21.320
Many skills are
needed to survive up here.

165
00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.760
So how does a cub learn those?

166
00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:32.200
Be a copycat of course.

167
00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:37.760
What mom does, son does.

168
00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.200
They're inseparable.

169
00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:51.440
But there's a serious
side to mountain life.

170
00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:54.360
And a constant
pressure to find food.

171
00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:57.960
Even at the top
of the food chain,

172
00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:01.400
survival up here is tough.

173
00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:04.640
Mother's milk
is no longer enough.

174
00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.120
She needs to hunt
every ten days or so

175
00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:11.080
just to keep
them both alive.

176
00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:15.120
But where is the prey?

177
00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:20.080
Finding it is the first job,
up here in the mountains.

178
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.480
Especially challenging
as a solitary hunter.

179
00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:29.480
Where one leopard's
territory might be

180
00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:33.560
as much as 1,000
square kilometers.

181
00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:39.520
About the size of Rome.

182
00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:43.520
And if she does find food,
it's difficult to sneak up on

183
00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.840
it because there just
aren't enough places to hide.

184
00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.680
These mountains help protect
an opposite universe from

185
00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:58.880
the cold air of the north.

186
00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:02.120
Further south, there's
a subtropical paradise where

187
00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:06.520
animals have more
than enough places to hide.

188
00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:10.520
So much so, they
need to up their game

189
00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:14.160
to even get noticed at all.

190
00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:18.920
The jungle of Yunnan.

191
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:21.760
Covering an area
larger than California,

192
00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:27.520
it's home to over half of the
entire country's plant species.

193
00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:33.240
It's a hidden kingdom that
shouldn't really be there.

194
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.800
Deep narrow valleys
funnel in warm, wet air,

195
00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:44.600
making it one of the most
northern jungles in the world.

196
00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:54.680
A place where leaves
and branches host some
outlandish life forms.

197
00:16:54.760 --> 00:17:00.160
With names that sound
like characters plucked
out of a comic book.

198
00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:05.120
These little caterpillars
are simply marvels of nature.

199
00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.600
This is Chequers,

200
00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:11.960
named after the geometric
pattern down its back.

201
00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:17.600
Shaped like a shield,
this one's called,

202
00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:21.280
funnily enough, the Shield.

203
00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:26.040
With a prominent,
nut-like lump in his middle,

204
00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:28.720
this is Almond-back.

205
00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:34.600
And dressed to
impress, the Jester.

206
00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:39.280
They may look cute
but chemical weapons,

207
00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:42.880
camouflage and deadly spines
are all tools in the arsenal

208
00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.040
of survival for
these jungle caterpillars.

209
00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:51.040
Thousands of tiny spines
cover this species called

210
00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:54.000
The Pin Cushion.

211
00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:56.840
The pink bumps on
its rear inflate and

212
00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:00.320
contain even
tinier pins to deter

213
00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:04.720
any predator that
crosses its path.

214
00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:13.080
Dragonhead emerges from
the egg, complete with horns,

215
00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:17.600
ready to defend itself.

216
00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:21.520
Reminiscent of
a Chinese dragon.

217
00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:28.480
The Snake Head wears
a mask to hide its head.

218
00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:33.280
While an airborne predator
might think it's a snake,

219
00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:38.000
this sneaky larva continues
to feed uninterrupted.

220
00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:44.880
But there's one costumed
caterpillar that predators
truly fear, Medusa.

221
00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:50.240
Locked and loaded with
a halo of stinging spines,

222
00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:54.240
it's all about defense.

223
00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:59.680
Each razor-sharp spike is
a hollow needle connected to

224
00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:04.680
a poison gland that's
ready to inject a toxin

225
00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:09.720
into anything unlucky
enough to come too close.

226
00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:17.880
Some caterpillars don't
need weapons to survive.

227
00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:22.320
They just sit
very, very, still.

228
00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.120
Like the Twig Caterpillar.

229
00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:28.640
With legs set far apart,

230
00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:32.680
it makes a characteristic
hump as it walks.

231
00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:36.800
Capable of remaining
motionless and undetected

232
00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:41.800
for days just like a twig.

233
00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:47.200
Or perhaps a home-built
fortress may suffice.

234
00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:51.320
Inside these leaves,
stuck together by her own silk,

235
00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:56.400
is a female caterpillar
known as the Witches Hat.

236
00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:58.880
To accommodate a growing body,

237
00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:04.800
this literal home maker adds
to her hat, leaf by tiny leaf.

238
00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.960
And she keeps a clean house.

239
00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:15.720
After gently balancing her
droppings on tiny silk threads,

240
00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:20.760
she jettisons
them as far as she can.

241
00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:27.480
Eventually she'll turn into
a wingless moth full of eggs.

242
00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:31.320
But for now she'll
stay in her cozy home,

243
00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:35.680
until a male moth comes
along to fertilize them.

244
00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:40.040
All these caterpillars
have the same simple goals,

245
00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:44.560
to eat and not be eaten.

246
00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:47.880
Stunning looks and
impressive adaptations go

247
00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:52.080
a long way in
the jungle of Yunnan.

248
00:20:55.640 --> 00:21:00.280
This rather handsome
devil is the Great Hornbill.

249
00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:05.080
Roughly the size of a
bald eagle, he's a big bird.

250
00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:10.240
He's also a loyal mate.

251
00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:13.560
Although what the female
hornbill does may seem to

252
00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:17.000
us very strange indeed.

253
00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:25.040
MICHELLE (off-screen):
This father hornbill
will be the sole provider

254
00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:28.920
for his family for
the next four months.

255
00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:32.440
But the mother
hornbill is holed up

256
00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:35.560
in this old tree
with her chicks.

257
00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:39.800
Trapped. 30 meters up.

258
00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.760
Completely relying
on him to feed her.

259
00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:50.480
She had to enter the nest
before laying her eggs,

260
00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:54.320
and shed her flight
feathers to make space
for a growing brood.

261
00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:01.560
Welcome to
motherhood hornbill style.

262
00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:07.760
It may seem extreme to us
but this is the jungle and

263
00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:12.200
you do what it takes
to protect your babies.

264
00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:19.600
Hornbills bond for life
and this pair is nesting in

265
00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:24.040
the same tree for
the tenth year straight.

266
00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:30.160
The mother's only outlet
to the world is a narrow gap

267
00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:33.320
within the tree hollow.

268
00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:36.920
Wide enough for the male
hornbill to supply food and

269
00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:41.280
narrow enough to
keep nest raiders out.

270
00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:56.080
Like a kind of
flying, candy machine,

271
00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:59.160
dad returns to the
nest many times a day with

272
00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:03.040
a beak full of treats.

273
00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:05.920
He stores the fruit in a
special pouch at the base of

274
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.520
his neck and to
pass it onto the female,

275
00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:14.000
he rolls each one out to
the tip of his massive beak.

276
00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:21.640
It's a highly skilled
and precision operation...

277
00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:26.160
most of the time.

278
00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:35.400
The male hornbill is
under intense pressure
to feed hungry mouths.

279
00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:48.560
Sugary fruit alone won't
be enough to sustain his young.

280
00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:54.080
This father has to
do some real hunting.

281
00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:59.840
A tiny owl chick will
make a good protein hit.

282
00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:04.200
But then.

283
00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:07.040
It's gone.

284
00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:11.280
The meal falls all the
way to the jungle floor.

285
00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:21.200
He has no choice but
to go out and hunt again.

286
00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:29.760
While the female waits
patiently with their brood.

287
00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:37.840
He snatches an
even bigger chick.

288
00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:41.200
A prize he
can't afford to lose.

289
00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:07.200
It's too big for the entrance.

290
00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:16.200
He crushes the bones
and he tries again.

291
00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.640
Still too big.

292
00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.640
But he's not giving up.

293
00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:30.640
That beak is
not just for show.

294
00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:42.000
Finally. Success.

295
00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:44.680
It'll be a while before
the hornbill chicks can leave

296
00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:49.240
their sanctuary high
up in the jungle tree.

297
00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:55.240
Trees provide more
than just shelter.

298
00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:00.440
Clean air, food.

299
00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:03.960
They are magnificent
silent heroes.

300
00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:08.280
And some of them are massive.

301
00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:12.440
This is a Banyan tree,

302
00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:19.040
300 years old and said to be
the largest in all of China.

303
00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:26.080
Out of its branches
thousands of aerial roots

304
00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:30.240
spread in all directions.

305
00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:34.880
From these, others sprout
and reach for the ground.

306
00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:39.240
Then they thicken
and form new trunks.

307
00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:44.480
The crown covers an
astonishing 8,000 square meters

308
00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:49.800
around the size of
a football field.

309
00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:56.000
This Banyan tree is so
big it makes its own forest.

310
00:26:57.920 --> 00:27:01.720
It's also sacred.

311
00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:08.640
Visitors leave red
colored offerings for
happiness and good luck.

312
00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:20.240
Meanwhile, back in the
bamboo forests of the east.

313
00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:23.480
The mother panda is getting
ready to introduce her baby

314
00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:27.440
to the big wide world.

315
00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:34.320
At just a few months old,
the baby panda still drinks

316
00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:38.640
his mom's milk
several times a day.

317
00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.640
But the older he gets,

318
00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:53.480
the more he can explore on his
own and learn about the forest.

319
00:27:56.360 --> 00:28:00.680
As long as his
mother is not too far away.

320
00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:17.920
Turns out, there's
plenty to discover in all the

321
00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:22.400
different parts
of the bamboo kingdom.

322
00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:26.800
And so many new
creatures to meet.

323
00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:42.080
Like this six-legged
alien predator.

324
00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:46.280
At just under five centimeters
long, she packs a mighty punch,

325
00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:49.720
an inspiration for
China's Kung Fu masters.

326
00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:04.360
MICHELLE (off-screen): This
female, jeweled flower mantis
is looking for flowers

327
00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:09.640
because they attract
her favorite prey,

328
00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:18.040
flies.

329
00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:27.720
Not here to chat,
they're here to drink nectar.

330
00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:42.640
But she's here to eat.

331
00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:53.000
And she's being watched.

332
00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:07.040
A smaller, male
mantis creeps closer.

333
00:30:09.320 --> 00:30:12.160
He's only after one thing.

334
00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:16.280
And it's not
the chewed-up fly.

335
00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:18.760
Even though
she's just eaten,

336
00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:22.960
he should still
tread carefully.

337
00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:31.520
A big beetle joins the party.

338
00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:46.880
Impressive.

339
00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:53.760
But now, back to business.

340
00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:06.960
He better be careful,

341
00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:14.200
a female praying mantis
will sometimes eat her man.

342
00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:28.480
These are the baby panda's
first steps to independence.

343
00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:39.920
Pandas are mostly loners
so these two years together

344
00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:43.200
with mom are precious.

345
00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:50.880
(squeaking).

346
00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:58.400
He could be just one of
a handful of cubs she raises

347
00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:02.600
in her lifetime, so the
next stage is one of the most

348
00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:06.480
important for both of them.

349
00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:16.840
It's a different story
for the four fox cubs.

350
00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:24.160
Their mother has been gone
a long time and dad is left

351
00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:26.920
with the job of
watching them...

352
00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:34.720
and feeding them.

353
00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:49.680
These cubs are so
dependent on their parents.

354
00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:55.960
But the odds of
survival are slim,

355
00:32:56.040 --> 00:33:00.160
one out of five
fox cubs won't make it.

356
00:33:00.680 --> 00:33:05.560
(yipping).

357
00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:09.240
If things get really tough,
these four may form their

358
00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:13.800
own hierarchy, which won't
be good for the little sister.

359
00:33:20.320 --> 00:33:24.080
Fall arrives in China's
high mountain forests.

360
00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:31.200
At similar latitudes
to North America's
deciduous forests,

361
00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:34.720
these glorious colors
look very familiar.

362
00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:46.560
The new baby snub-nosed monkey
is growing stronger every day.

363
00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:52.160
She's six months old
but still needs her mom.

364
00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:05.320
Experimentation and discovery
have begun for this little one.

365
00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:11.920
Her first new tastes,

366
00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:15.480
and her inaugural
attempts at grooming.

367
00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:24.120
But the best part,
she's confidently mobile.

368
00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:33.200
So now she spends a
lot of her time playing.

369
00:34:38.200 --> 00:34:42.000
It may be fun, but
like for most of us mammals,

370
00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:45.520
it's important too.

371
00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:50.000
Playing keeps this
little monkey fit and strong,

372
00:34:50.080 --> 00:34:54.720
gives her good social skills
and sharpens her brain too.

373
00:34:55.240 --> 00:34:58.400
(squeaking).

374
00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:02.240
As long as she
gets the landing right.

375
00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:08.360
In Chinese folklore,
monkeys symbolize
rebellion and mischief.

376
00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:12.360
It's no wonder.

377
00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:16.200
The troop's
bachelor gang fits the part.

378
00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:28.520
Families push out these young
males before they turn three.

379
00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:40.480
And boys will be boys.

380
00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.240
They're preparing for the
day when they'll fight to

381
00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:54.560
win their own family.

382
00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:07.760
One of the older males has
already reached that point.

383
00:36:10.840 --> 00:36:13.920
Aged seven, those
warts on his lips show that

384
00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:17.640
this bachelor is ready.

385
00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:23.600
Apparently, the little lumps
are attractive to females.

386
00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:26.920
But he can only
win the right to mate

387
00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:30.000
if he can take over a family.

388
00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:35.720
That means he must overthrow
the father of the baby monkey.

389
00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:43.240
MICHELLE: The alpha male
and his family are under threat

390
00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:46.600
in China's high
mountain forests.

391
00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:51.320
Other males may
kill the babies,

392
00:36:51.400 --> 00:36:53.680
if they succeed
in taking over.

393
00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:58.480
Simply so they can
sire their own offspring.

394
00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:04.840
(screaming).

395
00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:09.040
The little monkey's father
is an experienced fighter.

396
00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:18.520
He's not as strong
as he used to be,

397
00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:22.280
but his harem of
females has got his back.

398
00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:26.400
♪ ♪

399
00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:30.400
So, he still
has the upper hand.

400
00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:36.920
♪ ♪

401
00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:50.240
The challenger is sent packing,

402
00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:54.440
and father maintains his
position as head of the family.

403
00:37:56.560 --> 00:38:00.280
The females seem
happy to keep him there.

404
00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:05.000
He returns to the baby
monkey's mother for comfort.

405
00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:13.080
And because fall
is the breeding season,

406
00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:17.400
he's about to get some
extra looks from the girls.

407
00:38:20.480 --> 00:38:24.520
To win his affection they
shower him with attention.

408
00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:34.040
Female golden snub-nosed monkeys
use grooming as currency,

409
00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:38.120
hoping to exchange
it for romance.

410
00:38:44.280 --> 00:38:48.760
This one seems to be
finding all the right spots.

411
00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:56.000
♪ ♪

412
00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:02.440
Until the kids get in the way.

413
00:39:06.720 --> 00:39:10.240
They'll mate several times.

414
00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:17.240
And fingers crossed,
this female will
give birth next spring.

415
00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:30.800
As fall progresses in
China's mountain forests,

416
00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:34.200
trees continue
to shed their leaves.

417
00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:38.520
A strategy to save energy
during the cold months ahead.

418
00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:44.160
This means less food for
the monkeys although they make

419
00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:48.120
do with bamboo
leaves and pine needles.

420
00:39:53.720 --> 00:39:58.240
While all the other forest
creatures get ready for winter.

421
00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:03.760
Other animals take
colder temperatures in stride.

422
00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:11.360
They don't have a choice.

423
00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:15.320
High up in the mountains

424
00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:18.240
the snow leopard mother
and her cub cross into

425
00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:22.640
an area of steep cliffs
and crumbling rocks.

426
00:40:26.920 --> 00:40:30.640
Finding food
here is a challenge.

427
00:40:30.720 --> 00:40:34.440
But actually catching it?

428
00:40:34.520 --> 00:40:38.120
That's on another level.

429
00:40:38.200 --> 00:40:41.040
Literally.

430
00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:46.640
Hunting in these mountains
is a serious undertaking.

431
00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:54.160
These blue sheep are
the leopard's main target.

432
00:40:55.480 --> 00:40:59.240
And just like her, they're able
to disappear into the rocks.

433
00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:07.440
The sheep are expert
climbers on the steep cliffs,

434
00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:11.120
a skill that helps them access
one of their favorite foods,

435
00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:16.600
the energy-rich lichen.

436
00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:22.280
These high-altitude herbivores
are built for the job.

437
00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:24.960
Split hooves make
rock-climbing easier as they

438
00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:28.560
spread the animal's
weight for better balance.

439
00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:34.800
And rough pads under each toe
give them a suction-like grip.

440
00:41:36.600 --> 00:41:40.080
These sheep don't slip.

441
00:41:41.920 --> 00:41:44.440
A tough target for the
mother snow leopard,

442
00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:47.600
but worth the trouble.

443
00:41:51.080 --> 00:41:54.280
And if a sheep can
climb these rocks,

444
00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:57.840
then it shouldn't be
any problem for a cat.

445
00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:07.360
Still, practice
makes perfect.

446
00:42:12.160 --> 00:42:15.120
Patience too.

447
00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:19.760
The blue sheep don't just eat
lichen from the craggy cliffs.

448
00:42:19.840 --> 00:42:22.960
They also need to graze
the open slopes where they rely

449
00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:26.440
on strength in numbers.

450
00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:34.080
The terrain may
be easier here but there
are fewer places to hide.

451
00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:37.440
Mom and her cub need a
different hunting strategy

452
00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:40.160
if they're to succeed.

453
00:42:41.080 --> 00:42:44.520
The skill is in the ambush.

454
00:42:46.240 --> 00:42:49.840
Playfulness morphs
into hunting practice.

455
00:42:49.920 --> 00:42:53.520
The cub tricks his mother
but can he do it for real?

456
00:42:54.760 --> 00:42:58.160
The time has come
to put him to the test.

457
00:43:01.880 --> 00:43:04.520
MICHELLE: A small group
of blue sheep has moved away

458
00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:07.160
from the main herd.

459
00:43:07.240 --> 00:43:10.040
Ripe for the picking.

460
00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:15.880
The mother snow
leopard rolls in the dirt,

461
00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:19.800
perhaps to add to her camo.

462
00:43:24.320 --> 00:43:28.480
But her cub is in danger
of giving her position away.

463
00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:40.040
He's old enough now to know
to keep down low and stay out of

464
00:43:40.120 --> 00:43:44.080
the sheep's line of sight.

465
00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:51.160
Mom moves in from behind
with silence and stealth.

466
00:43:56.880 --> 00:44:00.160
The cub holds his position.

467
00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:06.000
They're in the perfect
place to make a kill.

468
00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:13.320
But the sheep are twitchy.

469
00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:19.720
The game is up.

470
00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:29.400
Bad luck this time but the
cub adds to his ambush bag of

471
00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:32.640
tricks for next time.

472
00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:47.480
Overnight, another hunter
takes down one of the sheep.

473
00:44:50.280 --> 00:44:54.040
Remote cameras capture
rare moments of predators and

474
00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:57.840
scavengers dining
on this precious meal.

475
00:44:57.920 --> 00:45:02.200
A golden eagle shares
the prize with a red fox.

476
00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:08.480
A Tibetan fox
grabs what he can.

477
00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:16.160
And then, another snow
leopard pair takes their
share of the carcass.

478
00:45:36.640 --> 00:45:39.840
50 million years ago,
the grasslands that cover

479
00:45:39.920 --> 00:45:45.360
China's Qinghai Tibet Plateau
were an ancient sea.

480
00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:51.320
But when the Himalayas were
formed they were thrust up,

481
00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:54.400
five kilometers high.

482
00:45:56.800 --> 00:46:00.920
(thunder).

483
00:46:01.000 --> 00:46:04.600
Today, melting glaciers from
the surrounding mountains

484
00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:06.880
create hundreds of lakes,

485
00:46:06.960 --> 00:46:10.280
which nourish
these great plains.

486
00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:15.640
Like the mighty Donggei Cuona.

487
00:46:17.840 --> 00:46:21.560
For most of the year
it's covered in ice,

488
00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:30.240
but during the summer
it becomes a precious oasis.

489
00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:39.040
Its waters help
support a diversity of life.

490
00:46:49.520 --> 00:46:55.440
These high grasslands
echo to the courtship duet
of very rare visitors.

491
00:46:55.520 --> 00:46:57.720
(squawking).

492
00:46:57.800 --> 00:47:00.400
Black-necked cranes.

493
00:47:01.360 --> 00:47:04.640
Almost the entire world
population of an estimated

494
00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:10.480
11,000 nest along the
shores of these great
lakes during the summer.

495
00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:15.640
(squawking).

496
00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:20.480
It's a safe place
to raise a family and
there's plenty of food.

497
00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:29.920
For centuries, Tibetans have
revered these birds as symbols

498
00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:34.960
of longevity and harmony
between people and nature.

499
00:47:40.760 --> 00:47:45.960
The Tibetan fox family
live by the lakeshore too.

500
00:47:47.560 --> 00:47:51.800
The four cubs are still
waiting for their mom.

501
00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:57.240
So dad has the full-time job
of supplying them with snacks.

502
00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:03.400
Thankfully the snacks
live within easy range.

503
00:48:06.120 --> 00:48:09.000
Pikas.

504
00:48:09.080 --> 00:48:12.240
They're related to rabbits
but are about the size of

505
00:48:12.320 --> 00:48:18.680
a guinea pig and make up to
95% of the Tibetan fox diet.

506
00:48:20.240 --> 00:48:22.680
They live in large,
underground colonies with

507
00:48:22.760 --> 00:48:25.800
hundreds of individuals.

508
00:48:25.880 --> 00:48:28.680
And, like most of
the vegetarians up here,

509
00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:33.120
spend most of the summer
grazing on the grass.

510
00:48:33.800 --> 00:48:38.640
They can eat as much as half
their body weight each day.

511
00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:47.520
Pikas may live in
underground burrows

512
00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:51.400
but the foxes have
got that covered.

513
00:48:52.320 --> 00:48:56.680
(chirping).

514
00:48:57.800 --> 00:49:00.040
(chirping, growling).

515
00:49:05.120 --> 00:49:07.800
(growling).

516
00:49:08.680 --> 00:49:11.680
Their extremely long canines
help them hook the little

517
00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:15.400
mammals from a tight spot.

518
00:49:15.480 --> 00:49:18.080
Big and powerful jaw
muscles make their heads

519
00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:21.840
a little larger than usual.

520
00:49:21.920 --> 00:49:26.600
The head also seems to
hide the rest of their body
when hunting in the open.

521
00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:40.720
The fox makes use of
the slightest contours of
the land for the ambush.

522
00:49:45.920 --> 00:49:49.600
This pika is grazing
very close to its burrow,

523
00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:52.440
too easy an escape.

524
00:49:52.520 --> 00:49:56.040
So, dad needs to get within
at least three meters to

525
00:49:56.120 --> 00:50:00.040
have any chance
of grabbing it.

526
00:50:02.200 --> 00:50:06.440
(chattering).

527
00:50:06.520 --> 00:50:11.840
He must catch around
20 pikas a day if he's
to satisfy his large brood.

528
00:50:15.600 --> 00:50:18.520
The cubs get what
they need for now,

529
00:50:18.600 --> 00:50:22.640
even little sis
snags her share.

530
00:50:23.920 --> 00:50:27.200
Dad's exhausted.

531
00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:35.320
Mother fox has
been gone for days.

532
00:50:35.400 --> 00:50:38.440
Where could she be?

533
00:50:39.960 --> 00:50:41.720
Little do they know,

534
00:50:41.800 --> 00:50:45.680
tragedy has struck
on China's grasslands.

535
00:50:46.040 --> 00:50:51.040
The fox cubs' mom has fallen
victim to Tibetan wolves.

536
00:50:52.840 --> 00:50:57.000
Father fox is
really on his own now.

537
00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:06.560
The hidden kingdoms of China
hold a whole other secret world.

538
00:51:06.640 --> 00:51:09.600
At night.

539
00:51:10.360 --> 00:51:14.440
No jungle ever really sleeps.

540
00:51:17.120 --> 00:51:20.280
In the subtropical
forests of Yunnan,

541
00:51:20.360 --> 00:51:23.680
another male resident
is on the prowl.

542
00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:27.040
For food?
Or for females?

543
00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:32.040
Either way, he takes
advantage of the dark
to get what he wants.

544
00:51:35.200 --> 00:51:37.920
This is a Slow Loris.

545
00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:42.800
Also known in
China as the Bee Monkey.

546
00:51:42.880 --> 00:51:46.520
The world's only venomous
primates have a bite that may

547
00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:50.320
protect them
against predators.

548
00:52:03.200 --> 00:52:06.200
They also have special blood
vessels in their wrists and

549
00:52:06.280 --> 00:52:09.680
ankles that deliver
oxygen to their muscles,

550
00:52:09.760 --> 00:52:14.880
allowing them to hang onto
branches for hours at a time.

551
00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:25.040
The slow loris is
a hunter of the night.

552
00:52:28.160 --> 00:52:31.720
Forward facing eyes give
him stereoscopic vision for

553
00:52:31.800 --> 00:52:35.200
pinpoint depth perception.

554
00:52:35.280 --> 00:52:38.240
And a reflective layer
behind the retina lets him

555
00:52:38.320 --> 00:52:44.000
see well in low light,
essential for a
nocturnal predator.

556
00:52:47.960 --> 00:52:50.520
Since most insects
are cold blooded,

557
00:52:50.600 --> 00:52:54.320
they tend to slow down
in the cool of the night.

558
00:52:54.400 --> 00:52:59.480
So, they play right into
the hands of the Bee Monkey.

559
00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:06.000
He's more about
stealth than speed.

560
00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:19.640
Tonight though,
he's distracted by her.

561
00:53:28.040 --> 00:53:32.760
But she's not in heat right
now and would rather stick

562
00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:36.840
to gobbling up bugs herself.

563
00:53:49.960 --> 00:53:54.080
Sorry darling not tonight.

564
00:53:58.880 --> 00:54:03.200
In China's bamboo forests
after dark there are other

565
00:54:03.280 --> 00:54:07.200
male creatures having
more luck with the girls.

566
00:54:07.280 --> 00:54:11.560
But be careful
what you wish for.

567
00:54:21.280 --> 00:54:25.200
MICHELLE (off-screen):
Meet the Emei mustache toad.

568
00:54:27.320 --> 00:54:32.280
16 sharp spines on his
nose defend his breeding site,

569
00:54:32.880 --> 00:54:36.800
(croaking).

570
00:54:39.080 --> 00:54:43.000
a smooth flat rock.

571
00:54:44.080 --> 00:54:48.480
This is where he wants multiple
females to lay their eggs.

572
00:54:48.560 --> 00:54:52.280
So far, he's been
quite successful.

573
00:54:52.360 --> 00:54:55.720
But he wants more.

574
00:54:59.240 --> 00:55:03.400
And he's not the only male
Emei toad playing this game.

575
00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:08.760
There's a rival close by
looking for his own love rock.

576
00:55:11.160 --> 00:55:14.360
(croaking).

577
00:55:14.440 --> 00:55:17.160
Perhaps using the
rock as an amplifier,

578
00:55:17.240 --> 00:55:20.880
he presses his back against
it to advertise his nest with

579
00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:23.480
a cacophony of croaks.

580
00:55:23.560 --> 00:55:25.480
(croaking).

581
00:55:25.560 --> 00:55:28.720
It's not enough though
he needs more volume.

582
00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:34.840
The other guy's rock
looks and sounds better.

583
00:55:34.920 --> 00:55:39.440
(croaking).

584
00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:44.440
(croaking).

585
00:55:46.920 --> 00:55:50.880
Time to take that
rock all for himself.

586
00:55:52.920 --> 00:55:55.440
It's a toad showdown.

587
00:55:56.680 --> 00:56:01.000
(croaking).

588
00:56:01.080 --> 00:56:04.240
But the first guy
has the upper hand.

589
00:56:04.760 --> 00:56:09.560
(croaking).

590
00:56:17.480 --> 00:56:22.320
He pushes him out
and keeps his rock.

591
00:56:29.200 --> 00:56:34.480
And it doesn't go unnoticed
by this gorgeous creature,

592
00:56:35.200 --> 00:56:37.280
a passing female.

593
00:56:37.360 --> 00:56:40.560
(croaking).

594
00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:44.840
What happens next has never
been filmed in the wild,

595
00:56:44.920 --> 00:56:49.080
the Emei Mustache
toad courtship dance.

596
00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:54.560
It starts with
a wave and a wink.

597
00:56:56.720 --> 00:57:02.080
His goal is to have her
lay her eggs next to those
left by previous females.

598
00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:10.240
But she takes some convincing.

599
00:57:10.320 --> 00:57:13.600
A neck rub, maybe?

600
00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:25.200
Back scratch?

601
00:57:25.280 --> 00:57:29.240
Whatever he's doing, it works.

602
00:57:35.200 --> 00:57:39.240
(croaking).

603
00:57:39.320 --> 00:57:43.840
She's ready to
release her eggs.

604
00:58:01.640 --> 00:58:05.960
Then, she presses herself
against the tiny eggs and

605
00:58:06.040 --> 00:58:09.840
glues them to his
carefully guarded rock.

606
00:58:16.240 --> 00:58:21.000
But there's a twist.

607
00:58:26.880 --> 00:58:30.360
Mother toad's
job here is done.

608
00:58:30.440 --> 00:58:33.800
She leaves Mr. Toad to look
after their brood until they

609
00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:37.200
hatch into tadpoles.

610
00:58:42.080 --> 00:58:46.240
The bamboo forest at night
hides many secret worlds under

611
00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:51.200
the rocks and
inside the bamboo.

612
00:58:52.280 --> 00:58:56.960
This species is
known as spiny bamboo.

613
00:58:57.040 --> 00:58:59.520
It grows to twice the
height of a telephone pole.

614
00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:02.240
And its stems are hollow.

615
00:59:02.320 --> 00:59:06.360
Some of them have tiny
slits made by beetles,

616
00:59:06.440 --> 00:59:10.920
born from eggs inside
the bamboo that have
since cut their way out.

617
00:59:14.520 --> 00:59:18.880
One animal takes
advantage of this real estate.

618
00:59:19.360 --> 00:59:21.520
At just a couple
of centimeters long,

619
00:59:21.600 --> 00:59:24.560
it's one of the
smallest mammals in the world.

620
00:59:28.600 --> 00:59:33.040
The lesser bamboo bat.

621
00:59:43.720 --> 00:59:49.640
One male shares a segment
of the bamboo stem with up to
12 females and their babies.

622
00:59:57.800 --> 01:00:00.640
The infants are
still too young to fly,

623
01:00:00.720 --> 01:00:05.960
so their parents leave
them alone while they go
out hunting for insects.

624
01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:09.160
Specially flattened skulls
help them squeeze their way

625
01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:13.520
out of slits just
a couple of centimeters wide.

626
01:00:24.600 --> 01:00:27.840
Even though the bats
hunt in short bursts,

627
01:00:27.920 --> 01:00:32.880
every minute they're away
leaves their babies vulnerable.

628
01:00:37.360 --> 01:00:42.520
A Chinese beauty rat
snake sniffs out a meal.

629
01:00:50.280 --> 01:00:55.160
That tongue can
sense live meat.

630
01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:05.840
The snake knows the bamboo
holds something good to eat.

631
01:01:12.880 --> 01:01:17.760
Reptilian eyes lock
in on the baby bats.

632
01:01:19.840 --> 01:01:26.200
(squeaking).

633
01:01:35.600 --> 01:01:39.320
But the bats know
what they're doing.

634
01:01:39.400 --> 01:01:43.960
The nest hole is
too narrow for a snake.

635
01:01:45.040 --> 01:01:48.800
Their babies
are safe, for now.

636
01:01:48.880 --> 01:01:52.880
At around a month old
they'll leave the nest.

637
01:01:52.960 --> 01:01:57.120
Let's hope the snake's
still not waiting.

638
01:02:03.920 --> 01:02:09.440
The queen of the bamboo
forest invests a lot more
time in her offspring.

639
01:02:12.480 --> 01:02:15.800
At eight months old,
he's still drinking her milk

640
01:02:15.880 --> 01:02:19.760
but he has started to take
an interest in other food.

641
01:02:19.840 --> 01:02:24.160
And when we say other food,
we really mean bamboo of course,

642
01:02:25.600 --> 01:02:28.200
and more bamboo.

643
01:02:28.280 --> 01:02:31.640
There's a lot for him
to learn about bamboo.

644
01:02:31.720 --> 01:02:36.400
What type and which
part is best to eat.

645
01:02:38.720 --> 01:02:42.720
The newer shoots are rich
in nitrogen and phosphorous.

646
01:02:42.800 --> 01:02:46.880
And the leaves
provide calcium.

647
01:02:48.400 --> 01:02:53.080
It's all good stuff.

648
01:02:55.880 --> 01:03:00.640
But then, the mother panda
does a fascinating thing

649
01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:03.800
that may add to her cub's diet.

650
01:03:08.480 --> 01:03:13.240
MICHELLE (off-screen): Something
rarely seen is underway in
China's bamboo forest.

651
01:03:14.680 --> 01:03:18.920
The mother panda encourages
her cub to lick her lips,

652
01:03:19.000 --> 01:03:21.920
which seems to
make her salivate.

653
01:03:25.400 --> 01:03:29.120
Then he drinks this saliva,

654
01:03:29.200 --> 01:03:33.400
possibly giving his
immune system a boost.

655
01:03:48.640 --> 01:03:54.680
By doing this, mom gives her cub
the best possible start in life

656
01:03:54.760 --> 01:04:00.240
but he still has at
least another year to go
under her watchful eye.

657
01:04:07.400 --> 01:04:11.800
With more confidence,
he becomes more adventurous.

658
01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:21.840
But he shouldn't
stray too far.

659
01:04:25.040 --> 01:04:29.960
Alone, baby
pandas are vulnerable.

660
01:04:31.160 --> 01:04:33.200
In some of China's forests,

661
01:04:33.280 --> 01:04:37.880
there are predators
like jackals and leopards.

662
01:04:40.320 --> 01:04:42.640
The cub's been
gone too long,

663
01:04:42.720 --> 01:04:45.920
the mother panda
needs to find him.

664
01:04:49.800 --> 01:04:51.480
With poor eyesight,

665
01:04:51.560 --> 01:04:55.480
pandas use their
heightened sense of smell
to locate each other.

666
01:05:02.640 --> 01:05:05.200
There he is.

667
01:05:05.280 --> 01:05:08.880
She's catches his
scent and calls to him.

668
01:05:12.120 --> 01:05:17.240
(panda bleating).

669
01:05:26.520 --> 01:05:31.520
This 90-kilogram mom
may look soft and cuddly,

670
01:05:33.320 --> 01:05:36.120
but she's more than
capable of fending off any

671
01:05:36.200 --> 01:05:39.480
predator that threatens her cub.

672
01:05:56.440 --> 01:06:01.840
♪ ♪

673
01:06:01.920 --> 01:06:06.640
In the mountain kingdom,
four seasons can
pass in one day.

674
01:06:08.960 --> 01:06:13.360
Snow even falls during summer,

675
01:06:15.760 --> 01:06:17.840
and a few hours later,

676
01:06:17.920 --> 01:06:22.120
temperatures can spike
as much as 15 degrees.

677
01:06:27.080 --> 01:06:31.680
The locals always
seem to find ways to cope.

678
01:06:33.040 --> 01:06:34.680
With her thick coat,

679
01:06:34.760 --> 01:06:37.960
the snow leopard is
certainly built for the job.

680
01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:42.800
But she's not the
only hardy feline that
makes a living up here.

681
01:06:45.840 --> 01:06:48.880
The Eurasian lynx.

682
01:06:53.120 --> 01:06:56.240
About two-thirds the weight
of her snow leopard cousin,

683
01:06:56.320 --> 01:07:00.280
she's a well-designed
mountain hunter.

684
01:07:02.360 --> 01:07:06.000
The hair on the top of
her ears look like antennae

685
01:07:06.080 --> 01:07:08.600
but really aid in her hearing,

686
01:07:08.680 --> 01:07:12.720
allowing her to tune in
to the slightest movement.

687
01:07:16.240 --> 01:07:20.280
Exceptional eyesight means
she can spot something as small

688
01:07:20.360 --> 01:07:24.320
as a mouse from 80 meters away.

689
01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:33.440
But probably the
hardiest creature up
here is not a predator.

690
01:07:34.680 --> 01:07:36.440
The yak.

691
01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:39.000
Perfectly equipped
for mountain life,

692
01:07:39.080 --> 01:07:41.800
their lung capacity
is about three times that

693
01:07:41.880 --> 01:07:45.720
of regular cattle
living at lower altitudes.

694
01:07:46.720 --> 01:07:50.640
When the yak eat, the
fermenting food generates heat.

695
01:07:51.520 --> 01:07:55.080
And this, together with their
thick fur, keeps them warm.

696
01:07:55.160 --> 01:07:57.520
Most of the time.

697
01:07:57.600 --> 01:08:02.760
They can still fall victim to
the mountain's harsh conditions.

698
01:08:04.480 --> 01:08:10.840
And a 350 kilogram
carcass out here will not
go unnoticed for long.

699
01:08:19.920 --> 01:08:23.480
A Tibetan blue bear.

700
01:08:23.560 --> 01:08:26.920
A cousin of the grizzly.

701
01:08:31.560 --> 01:08:35.480
She catches a whiff
of some yak carcasses.

702
01:08:38.880 --> 01:08:41.880
But she's not the only one.

703
01:08:41.960 --> 01:08:46.120
(howling).

704
01:08:46.200 --> 01:08:49.800
A pack of wolves does too.

705
01:08:53.800 --> 01:08:59.920
(howling).

706
01:09:03.840 --> 01:09:07.760
This bear is a mother
and with her cubs close by,

707
01:09:07.840 --> 01:09:11.800
these Tibetan wolves
are a serious threat.

708
01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:22.960
She still manages to
grab a piece of yak for them.

709
01:09:36.800 --> 01:09:40.040
(moaning).

710
01:09:40.120 --> 01:09:44.960
Thankfully, the wolves
focus on the rotting
meat not the baby bears.

711
01:09:48.960 --> 01:09:51.680
This food will
not only feed them,

712
01:09:51.760 --> 01:09:56.920
but also any of
their cubs that may be
waiting back at the den.

713
01:09:58.640 --> 01:10:02.360
More and more of the
pack enter the fray.

714
01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:10.240
By rubbing themselves
on the carcass,

715
01:10:10.320 --> 01:10:13.640
they can bring the scent
of food back to the den so

716
01:10:13.720 --> 01:10:18.200
the rest of the pack
knows where to feed later.

717
01:10:23.160 --> 01:10:28.600
(growling).

718
01:10:29.160 --> 01:10:32.760
The wolves take
all they need for now.

719
01:10:36.080 --> 01:10:40.400
While the cleanup crew waits
patiently for the scraps.

720
01:10:50.360 --> 01:10:54.600
The Eurasian lynx also
catches herself a meal.

721
01:10:54.680 --> 01:10:57.360
A Tibetan gazelle.

722
01:10:57.440 --> 01:11:00.880
But it's only half the job.

723
01:11:00.960 --> 01:11:04.920
She now has to move
the carcass somewhere safe.

724
01:11:10.040 --> 01:11:15.880
♪ ♪

725
01:11:17.040 --> 01:11:20.320
When the prey is nearly
as large as the predator,

726
01:11:20.400 --> 01:11:23.240
the challenge is on.

727
01:11:25.920 --> 01:11:31.440
Dragging it up an almost
vertical cliff has
to be a Herculean task.

728
01:11:32.600 --> 01:11:36.000
And there's always
someone else, hovering.

729
01:11:36.080 --> 01:11:39.280
Waiting for the
opportunity of a free meal.

730
01:11:45.480 --> 01:11:47.320
MICHELLE (off-screen): From
above, the ever-present,

731
01:11:47.400 --> 01:11:51.120
airborne scavengers
detect the lynx's kill.

732
01:11:54.120 --> 01:12:00.280
Soaring on the updrafts
created by steep cliffs,
Lammergeyers.

733
01:12:00.360 --> 01:12:05.400
With a wingspan of
nearly three meters long
and eyesight so strong,

734
01:12:05.480 --> 01:12:08.520
they can see a dead body
from seven kilometers away.

735
01:12:08.600 --> 01:12:12.600
They can be on it in minutes.

736
01:12:12.680 --> 01:12:16.120
And the lynx knows it.

737
01:12:19.120 --> 01:12:24.280
She drags the carcass
somewhere secure, she thinks.

738
01:12:31.520 --> 01:12:34.120
But then?

739
01:12:34.200 --> 01:12:37.160
Disaster.

740
01:12:45.440 --> 01:12:49.280
The cat watches her
hard-earned dinner tumble

741
01:12:49.360 --> 01:12:53.400
30 meters down the
steep mountain slope.

742
01:13:15.600 --> 01:13:20.800
She has no other option,
but to try again.

743
01:13:23.960 --> 01:13:29.600
And as fast
as possible before those
dreaded scavengers take it.

744
01:13:39.520 --> 01:13:43.840
She hauls the carcass
back up for a second time.

745
01:13:46.480 --> 01:13:51.240
It takes every last
ounce of her energy.

746
01:13:53.640 --> 01:13:56.880
But she does it.

747
01:14:01.400 --> 01:14:05.760
And this time she feasts.

748
01:14:16.040 --> 01:14:21.000
The mother snow leopard and
her cub also score a meal.

749
01:14:30.440 --> 01:14:34.680
This queen of the mountains
has successfully kept her cub

750
01:14:34.760 --> 01:14:39.080
alive in an extreme habitat.

751
01:14:42.400 --> 01:14:45.360
In the next few months,
he will be ready to go it

752
01:14:45.440 --> 01:14:49.600
alone in this
hidden mountain kingdom.

753
01:15:03.760 --> 01:15:08.560
On China's high plains,
things still look bleak for

754
01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:12.280
our Tibetan fox family.

755
01:15:12.360 --> 01:15:15.640
The younger sister
is getting weaker and

756
01:15:15.720 --> 01:15:19.280
may not survive
to see winter.

757
01:15:19.360 --> 01:15:24.400
The short window of summer
on these grasslands gives life

758
01:15:24.480 --> 01:15:28.000
but it also takes it.

759
01:15:34.080 --> 01:15:38.440
Father fox is
running on empty.

760
01:15:38.520 --> 01:15:41.440
But something quite
dramatic is about to happen

761
01:15:41.520 --> 01:15:44.800
that may just change
the fox family's fate.

762
01:15:45.320 --> 01:15:48.080
(bleating).

763
01:15:48.160 --> 01:15:52.560
Temporary visitors arrive
on these high, rich pastures.

764
01:15:53.320 --> 01:16:00.040
(bleating).

765
01:16:00.960 --> 01:16:06.000
Tibetan herders bring
their livestock here

766
01:16:06.080 --> 01:16:08.880
to fatten up on
the endless grass,

767
01:16:12.920 --> 01:16:16.920
like their ancestors have
done for thousands of years.

768
01:16:23.160 --> 01:16:28.440
The grassland transforms
and the fox cubs face
a completely different world.

769
01:16:28.880 --> 01:16:33.240
(bleating).

770
01:16:38.440 --> 01:16:43.640
Their home is now
swamped in sheep and yak.

771
01:16:47.040 --> 01:16:49.800
(bleating).

772
01:16:49.880 --> 01:16:53.760
And they're
cramping dad's style.

773
01:16:58.760 --> 01:17:01.840
They could make potential prey

774
01:17:01.920 --> 01:17:06.080
but they're a
little out of his league.

775
01:17:07.560 --> 01:17:10.560
Now, every time he
hunts, these strange,

776
01:17:10.640 --> 01:17:14.000
woolly creatures
blow his cover.

777
01:17:15.600 --> 01:17:19.840
(bleating).

778
01:17:28.160 --> 01:17:32.840
And the massive yak
are just big bullies.

779
01:17:34.840 --> 01:17:38.520
But this cunning
fox has a plan.

780
01:17:43.840 --> 01:17:47.280
MICHELLE (off-screen):
Father fox is patient.

781
01:17:47.360 --> 01:17:50.040
He's figured out
if he just waits,

782
01:17:50.120 --> 01:17:53.320
the sheep will
get used to him.

783
01:17:57.320 --> 01:18:00.280
And then he can use
them to his advantage.

784
01:18:00.360 --> 01:18:03.760
Kind of like a
mobile hunting blind.

785
01:18:08.200 --> 01:18:12.600
The pikas grow
complacent and are now
missing the danger signs.

786
01:18:19.680 --> 01:18:24.000
(bleating).

787
01:18:28.760 --> 01:18:34.000
Tibetan foxes are
intelligent and adaptable

788
01:18:34.080 --> 01:18:36.360
and that makes them, hands down,

789
01:18:36.440 --> 01:18:40.840
the most successful
hunters on China's grasslands.

790
01:18:52.680 --> 01:18:57.200
In fact, the father fox
is becoming too successful.

791
01:18:57.280 --> 01:19:01.520
So many pikas
but only one mouth.

792
01:19:04.840 --> 01:19:09.840
At last, there's more than
enough food for all four cubs.

793
01:19:09.920 --> 01:19:13.360
Even for the
smallest of the litter.

794
01:19:23.920 --> 01:19:28.200
Dad catches so many pikas,
he and his cubs can bury the

795
01:19:28.280 --> 01:19:31.840
leftovers in their
underground pantries.

796
01:19:32.920 --> 01:19:35.800
It keeps the meat
cool and preserved,

797
01:19:35.880 --> 01:19:40.040
a crucial supply
of food as summer ends.

798
01:19:43.520 --> 01:19:46.920
As the days close in
and the temperature drops,

799
01:19:47.000 --> 01:19:50.720
China's grasslands shut down.

800
01:19:53.040 --> 01:19:57.600
Residents retreat to
the shelter of their burrows.

801
01:20:00.840 --> 01:20:04.680
Visitors move out
to warmer climes.

802
01:20:12.200 --> 01:20:17.960
The Tibetan foxes
grow thick fur coats in
preparation for winter.

803
01:20:21.640 --> 01:20:26.040
Dad's dedication to his family
means that miraculously

804
01:20:26.120 --> 01:20:30.120
all of his cubs make it through.

805
01:20:31.120 --> 01:20:35.120
Four more months and
they will be on their own.

806
01:20:38.480 --> 01:20:44.160
But for now, the grasslands
vanish beneath the snow.

807
01:20:46.120 --> 01:20:50.040
And as the winter temperatures
in the high mountain forests

808
01:20:50.120 --> 01:20:54.400
barely stay above freezing,
the baby snub-nosed monkey

809
01:20:54.480 --> 01:20:58.160
seeks comfort and
warmth with her mother.

810
01:21:01.360 --> 01:21:05.520
She's now nine months old.

811
01:21:06.720 --> 01:21:10.440
These monkeys tolerate the
coldest average temperatures

812
01:21:10.520 --> 01:21:14.720
of any non-human
primate on the planet.

813
01:21:17.440 --> 01:21:21.440
And there's no escape from it.

814
01:21:22.240 --> 01:21:27.200
Fortunately, their
dense golden coats are
designed for the cold.

815
01:21:27.280 --> 01:21:31.160
It even thickens up
for the winter months,

816
01:21:32.040 --> 01:21:35.720
and covers their hands and feet.

817
01:21:38.440 --> 01:21:43.200
And here's where those odd,
snub noses come into play,

818
01:21:43.280 --> 01:21:47.240
they're simply less
exposed to frost bite.

819
01:21:48.480 --> 01:21:54.080
These monkeys also have
the most extraordinary
adaptation for the winter.

820
01:22:06.440 --> 01:22:10.560
Golden snub-nosed monkeys are
the only monkeys in the world

821
01:22:10.640 --> 01:22:15.960
known to walk upright
on two legs in snow.

822
01:22:19.840 --> 01:22:23.560
It keeps their hands
off the cold ground.

823
01:22:28.880 --> 01:22:32.440
And helps them travel faster,

824
01:22:32.520 --> 01:22:37.320
reducing the time they spend
in contact with the icy snow.

825
01:22:41.520 --> 01:22:44.720
Our new baby reaches
another milestone,

826
01:22:44.800 --> 01:22:49.160
as she gets the
hang of this new skill.

827
01:22:56.400 --> 01:23:01.840
These monkeys could
also be the source of
an ancient Chinese legend.

828
01:23:06.920 --> 01:23:11.960
These forests are said to
be home to mysterious, wild men.

829
01:23:14.760 --> 01:23:18.640
Ape-like figures
with red hair,

830
01:23:18.720 --> 01:23:23.760
who walk upright
through the trees.

831
01:23:25.320 --> 01:23:29.600
Sounds suspiciously like
the golden snub-nosed monkey.

832
01:23:33.880 --> 01:23:37.600
Or not.

833
01:23:41.920 --> 01:23:45.440
After months pass
and winter finally breaks

834
01:23:45.520 --> 01:23:49.640
in the high mountain
forests, the snow melts.

835
01:23:49.720 --> 01:23:52.760
And these high life
primates can start eating

836
01:23:52.840 --> 01:23:56.080
their favorite food again.

837
01:23:59.160 --> 01:24:03.880
They'll soon get back
the body weight they lost.

838
01:24:22.360 --> 01:24:27.960
The baby is now a year old,

839
01:24:28.960 --> 01:24:33.880
but she still returns to her
mother for milk and comfort.

840
01:24:39.200 --> 01:24:41.960
She won't be fully
weaned for another year,

841
01:24:42.040 --> 01:24:45.840
when her mom
gives birth again.

842
01:24:46.840 --> 01:24:51.840
But they'll stay close in
the family for years to come.

843
01:24:55.160 --> 01:24:59.200
She also has a
new little cousin.

844
01:24:59.840 --> 01:25:05.760
No longer the baby,
she now has someone she
can teach and care for.

845
01:25:14.960 --> 01:25:20.160
There's always time
to monkey around in China's
hidden forest kingdom.

846
01:25:22.640 --> 01:25:25.400
As for the bamboo kingdom...

847
01:25:28.400 --> 01:25:31.320
although not as
nimble as a monkey,

848
01:25:31.400 --> 01:25:36.280
the panda cub still needs
to learn how to climb a tree.

849
01:25:38.400 --> 01:25:43.720
Choosing the right
one is half the battle.

850
01:25:45.120 --> 01:25:48.920
One that will suit
his size and weight.

851
01:25:54.200 --> 01:25:58.400
He's going to have
to learn the hard way.

852
01:26:01.600 --> 01:26:04.840
(cracking, crashing).

853
01:26:10.280 --> 01:26:15.080
First, he needs to find
the right tree for him.

854
01:26:15.840 --> 01:26:19.800
And then get a good grip.

855
01:26:39.360 --> 01:26:41.480
He makes it.

856
01:26:41.560 --> 01:26:45.960
Up here, he gets a whole
new perspective on life.

857
01:26:52.400 --> 01:26:56.320
And as time goes by
in China's bamboo kingdom,

858
01:26:56.400 --> 01:27:00.640
the panda cub is finally
ready to leave his mother.

859
01:27:01.320 --> 01:27:05.920
He's two now and has grown
into a powerful young bear,

860
01:27:06.000 --> 01:27:09.240
weighing in at 90 kilograms,

861
01:27:09.320 --> 01:27:13.160
about as heavy as his mom.

862
01:27:16.040 --> 01:27:20.560
He's on the cusp
of independence,

863
01:27:20.640 --> 01:27:24.800
and the bond with his
mother couldn't be stronger.

864
01:27:29.840 --> 01:27:33.680
But there comes a stage in
every panda mother's life when

865
01:27:33.760 --> 01:27:38.760
she must let go and encourage
her young one out of the nest.

866
01:27:54.120 --> 01:27:59.760
He faces an exciting
and promising life ahead

867
01:27:59.840 --> 01:28:04.480
with the whole of China's
magical bamboo kingdom

868
01:28:04.560 --> 01:28:07.880
to discover for himself.

869
01:28:09.120 --> 01:28:11.880
And he doesn't look back.

870
01:28:15.040 --> 01:28:16.280
Captioned by
Cotter Captioning Services.
